export anchor TestExplanation:
    description:
        A test judged by an AI can fail for reasons that are hard to see from
        one sentence, so a failure also asks for an explanation, written in
        ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English: a controlled language made to
        be read quickly, and without ambiguity, also by readers whose first
        language isn't English. It is requested in the same session as the
        verdict, so it costs no extra run.

        The request gives the rules that matter most in a few sentences. At most
        four sentences, on the verdict's one line. Only words from the
        ASD-STE100 dictionary with their approved meanings, plus technical names
        like file, anchor and property names, and one word for one meaning.
        Sentences of no more than 20 words, with one topic each. The active
        voice and the simple present, past or future tense. No -ing forms of
        verbs, contractions or phrasal verbs. Articles where they belong, and
        noun clusters of three words or fewer. It says what is missing or
        different, and where.

        The explanation is the verdict's explanation, kept only when pass is
        false. A failure without one still fails, and shows only its reason.
        Asking for ASD-STE100 is not checking it: the text is shown as the tool
        wrote it.
